Sorting algorithms arrange elements in a specific order. Bubble sort and insertion sort are simple sorting algorithms that are easy to understand but less efficient for large datasets. Bubble sort repeatedly compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. Insertion sort builds a sorted sublist by repeatedly inserting elements from the unsorted portion into the correct position in the sorted sublist.

Bubble sort has a time complexity of O(n^2), while insertion sort has a time complexity of O(n^2) in the worst case but can be O(n) in the best case (when the data is already sorted). These algorithms are suitable for small datasets or educational purposes.

```python
# Example: Bubble Sort
def bubble_sort(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(0, n-i-1):
            if arr[j] > arr[j+1]:
                arr[j], arr[j+1] = arr[j+1], arr[j]

# Example: Insertion Sort
def insertion_sort(arr):
    for i in range(1, len(arr)):
        key = arr[i]
        j = i-1
        while j >= 0 and key < arr[j] :
                arr[j + 1] = arr[j]
                j -= 1
        arr[j + 1] = key
```
